From: OpsRemote
Subject: Windows: no null dereference when a thread with thread_local Qt data ends (MinGW)

GCC on MinGW destroys emulated thread_local storage before the C++
thread_local destructors run, so destroy_current_thread_data() receives
nullptr from ~Cleanup when a QThread ends. The fault is caught by Windows
during thread exit and reported silently through Windows Error Reporting,
which freezes the process for about a second and shows a busy pointer.
See msys2/MINGW-packages#2519.
---
--- a/src/corelib/thread/qthread_win.cpp	2026-11-03 23:22:31.940195996 +0200
+++ b/src/corelib/thread/qthread_win.cpp	2026-11-03 23:22:31.952043388 +0200
@@ -53,6 +53,13 @@
 static void destroy_current_thread_data(void *p)
 {
     QThreadData *data = static_cast<QThreadData *>(p);
+    // MinGW (GCC's emulated TLS) frees thread_local storage before it runs
+    // the thread_local destructors, so ~Cleanup below reads nullptr here when
+    // a QThread ends (msys2/MINGW-packages#2519). Nothing is left to clean up
+    // then; dereferencing it faulted, and Windows Error Reporting's silent
+    // report of that froze the whole process for about a second.
+    if (!data)
+        return;
     QThread *thread = data->thread.loadAcquire();
 
     if (data->isAdopted) {
